  "narrative": "Signed convention used throughout: for direction -1, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a stronger, more negative dependency score than the comparator; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a weaker, less negative dependency score. Clinical significance was assessed against a roughly 10 percent relative-difference scale for each outcome; for these dependency scores this corresponds to absolute effects on the order of 0.009 to 0.042, whereas the accepted large dependency programs have estimates near 0.78 to 0.82, many multiples above threshold. All claims below are dataset-specific association statements, not causal or externally generalizable claims. H9, MSI-high versus MSI-non-high WRN dependency direction -1, received validation R103: signed +0.8218 with adjusted interval +0.7685 to +0.8752 in cells of 105 exposed and 1895 comparator, alongside discovery R9 of +0.7899. The validation interval excludes null and has a lower bound more than twenty-fold above the approximate WRN clinical scale of about 0.030. This strongly supports the core supplied-dataset association that MSI-high lines show much stronger WRN dependency. I therefore accept and close H9 as the exact comparison resolved by independent validation. H10, MYC amplification versus no MYC amplification for DHX9 dependency direction -1, is now decisively unsupported: discovery R10 was negative and clinically null, and validation R77 was signed -0.0264 with adjusted interval -0.0595 to +0.0066, again opposite to the claimed stronger-DHX9-dependency direction and not supporting a clinically meaningful positive effect. I reject and close H10. H95, NF1 loss versus NF1 wild type among KRAS and BRAF wild type cells for KRAS dependency direction +1, had a favorable discovery signal R65 but failed independent validation: R76 was signed -0.0161 with adjusted interval -0.0621 to +0.0299, crossing null and pointing the opposite direction from the claimed weaker KRAS dependency. The exact selected context therefore does not replicate, so I reject and close H95. This downgrades the NF1 bypass-to-KRAS-dependency program; broader NF1 claims remain unresolved and low priority rather than supported. H106, TP53 loss versus TP53 wild type among adenocarcinoma KRAS wild type cells for MDM2 dependency direction +1, is also rejected: discovery R75 was near null, and validation R104 was signed -0.0301 with adjusted interval -0.0808 to +0.0206, crossing null and opposite to the claimed weaker MDM2 dependency. This exact MDM2 TP53-loss context fails independent validation, so the canonical TP53-loss weaker-MDM2-dependency candidate is not supported here and is deprioritized. Overall, the supported evidence continues to be dominated by four large supplied-dataset dependency programs: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, after the original opposite-direction claim was rejected; and TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency. Several of these have independent validation, including WRN and USP7 now, and many context analyses show large effects across co-mutational, batch, screening-quality, and subtype subsets. The main negative findings now include failed validation for MYC-DHX9, NF1-loss KRAS dependency, PTEN-loss EGFR dependency in the best selected high-Cas9 KRAS wild type adenocarcinoma context, and TP53-loss MDM2 dependency in the adenocarcinoma KRAS wild type context, plus specificity nulls for POLR2A broad shifts and several non-target controls. Remaining uncertainty is concentrated in weaker candidate programs, including EGFR-amplification and KRAS-mutation associations, KIF18A TP53-loss signals, POLR2A specificity controls, ARID1A interaction precision, marker negative controls, and the high-Cas9 MDM2 refinement. The most informative next analyses are to test whether the four headline large dependency associations remain very large specifically in high Cas9 activity and Avana-library screens; persistence would reduce concern that screening-library or Cas9-quality confounding drives the headline signals, whereas attenuation would identify an important technical context boundary.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 8 synthesis update. Signed convention remains in force. Validation response: H9 MSI-high WRN succeeded with R103 +0.8218, lower bound +0.7685, accept and close. H10 MYC-DHX9 failed validation and remains rejected. H95 NF1-loss KRAS dependency failed validation and is rejected/closed, downgrading the NF1 KRAS bypass program. H106 MDM2 TP53-loss adenocarcinoma KRAS-wt failed validation and is rejected/closed; TP53-MDM2 is deprioritized. Supported dataset-specific large programs remain SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and TP53-loss weaker USP7 dependency. PTEN EGFR remains downgraded after H105 failure; related PTEN contexts remain unresolved/deferred unless new strong evidence appears. Unresolved lower-priority programs include EGFR amplification, KRAS mutation, KIF18A TP53-loss, POLR2A broad-shift specificity tests, ARID1A interaction H21, MDM2 high-Cas9 H138, marker controls H78/H79, and various accepted-signal context completeness claims. Next planned analyses: test all four headline large effects under cas9_activity_score >=0.846 and crispr_library_avana=1 to assess library and screening-quality robustness. No additional validation requested at synthesis; future validation should be reserved for any new clinically-scale signal that remains strong after these robustness tests."
